  "Number": 17,
  "Question": "Suppose that \\(\\sin a+\\sin b=\\sqrt{\\tfrac53}\\) and \\(\\cos a+\\cos b=1.\\) What is \\(\\cos(a-b)?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\sqrt{\\dfrac53}-1\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac13\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac12\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac23\\)",
    "E": "\\(1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The identity\n\\[\n\\cos(a-b)=\\cos a\\cos b+\\sin a\\sin b\n\\]\nshows which products we want. Squaring the given sums creates these products. Adding the squares also lets us use \\(\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta=1\\) for each angle:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n& (\\sin a+\\sin b)^2+(\\cos a+\\cos b)^2\\\\\n&=2+2(\\sin a\\sin b+\\cos a\\cos b)\\\\\n&=2+2\\cos(a-b).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFrom the given values, the same sum is \\(5/3+1=8/3\\). Therefore\n\\[\n2+2\\cos(a-b)=\\frac83,\n\\qquad \\cos(a-b)=\\boxed{\\frac13}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice B.",
